Hmm: `letter-spacing: -0.03em` — negative em; fine.

Wait, the class `text-cp-critical` from band.tailwind sets color too — both set color; Tailwind utility comes later in cascade? Tailwind utilities are in @layer utilities (via @tailwind utilities at top of file), and my .cp-verdict__label is a plain rule outside layers → plain rules win over @layer rules regardless of order? In CSS cascade layers, unlayered styles have higher priority than layered ones ✓. Tailwind's utilities are in layers (base/components/utilities) → my unlayered rules win. So band.tailwind class would be overridden by .cp-verdict__label color.

That's a problem: band.tailwind must actually apply. Where do I rely on band.tailwind? In VerdictBadge I apply `${band.tailwind}` to label and runway spans. If .cp-verdict__label sets color: var(--cp-band) it overrides. The visual result is identical (same color), but the tailwind field becomes decorative.To make tailwind meaningful, I should NOT set color in .cp-verdict__label/.cp-verdict__runway (let the utility do it), and use --cp-band only for glyph/border. Then band.tailwind is the single source of color for text ✓ and the tailwind class is genuinely used (and scanned by Tailwind content ✓).

But then the compact/hero variants both rely on utility classes ✓. And if someone forgets the class, text is default ink. Fine.

